  "speechText": "Yeah. You and me both.\n\nThat depends. Does the room have a bed?\n\nThen yes. Definitely me.\n\nI've lived with worse.\n\nIf I unpack, it means I'm staying. If I stay, somebody will eventually ask why I came. And what am I supposed to tell them? That I drove halfway across the country because every street back home still knows his name?\n\nHe was being an idiot.\n\nHe took my bag.\n\nI took it back.\n\nShut up.\n\nYou're pulling my leg off.\n\nI wasn't scared.\n\nYou're stupid.\n\nCan I punch you now?\n\nThat's not my age,\n\nI'm ten.\n\nMy birthday. My rules.\n\nGood teacher.\n\nWhy are you out here?\n\nFrom what?\n\nThere aren't any spiders.\n\nWhy would we want them to slam?\n\nI'm already annoyed with you.\n\nWonderful. Saves me explaining.\n\nDid the van also break your phone?\n\nFinally, a diagnosis.\n\nDid you get extra chips?\n\nYes.\n\nBecause you know how it ends.\n\nAt midnight? You don't even take this route.\n\nWhat's that?\n\nI pay my own rent.\n\nThen why are you paying it?\n\nCass.\n\nWhere did it come from?\n\nWhat work?\n\nYou'd complain about boring work. You complain when your socks are too boring.\n\nI don't need you to buy me a life.\n\nThen tell me what you are doing.\n\nWhat's funny?\n\nWell, you've heard it. It remains incredibly charming.\n\nCome over Sunday. I'll cook.\n\nA drunk one?\n\nWhere did you even find this?\n\nYou have mysterious debts, probably.\n\nYou okay?\n\nThat sounded rehearsed.\n\nCall me tomorrow,\n\nAnd don't do anything stupid.\n\nI always do.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhat happened?\n\nKilled?\n\nWhere?\n\nWas he alone?\n\nDid he call anyone? Did he say anything?\n\nWhen can I see him?\n\nYou said you'd call tomorrow. You always called. I don't understand how the world can keep moving when you've stopped. I don't know what I'm supposed to do with all the things I haven't told you.\n\nHe's smiling like that because someone dared him to eat everything,\n\nHe was,\n\nAbout what?\n\nWhat would you like me to say?\n\nI know what you thought.\n\nCass, this is disgusting.\n\nWell, Cass,\n\nSomewhere nobody knows him. That's all I asked for. One evening where I can just be the woman ordering a drink, instead of the woman with the dead brother.\n\nWhiskey, please.\n\nThe one that doesn't ask personal questions.\n\nThen your favorite.\n\nWho are you?\n\nYou look like the kind of man who has an answer prepared for everything, and not one of them is the truth.\n\nDon't start. Don't make somebody else's silence into your problem.\n\nSo why haven't you looked away?",
